 "open_questions": [
  {
   "question": "Q1. How does a registration's child-list reading reach the tier? Today 65 child-rendering registrations (+ sidebar and sidebar-menu-button in context) publish it nowhere the tier reads, and 15 of them publish it only through isContainer.",
   "options": [
    "A · declare-and-pin. Every renderer that reads schema.children declares `{ name: 'children', type: 'slot' }`, the shape 10 registrations already carry (tooltip, page:card/footer/section/sidebar, the 5 page kinds). The tier's containment reads only that input. The existing runtime census is re-pointed from the flag to the input in both directions: the red-when-fixed pins flip to assert the new truth, and the `excluded`/`undeclared` rows are deleted as they clear. Four axes. ① Business: covers the 55 runtime violators plus tooltip, sidebar and sidebar-menu-button, which is the measured pull. ② Long-term: one published field (`inputs`) is read by the tier, the save gate and the docs table. It is still a declaration, held by an executed census rather than derived from code. ③ AI-error: the diagnostic stops lying on the only spelling that renders, with no flag fallback. A slot input emits no JSX attribute (read from codegen.ts emitInterface), so enforcement stays diagnostic-only. ④ Startup: no new field, no new gate (it re-points the existing ratchet). Cost: about 67 mechanical registration edits across components and layout, well outside the claimed file surface; the `inputs` of 9 public blocks grow in sdui.manifest.json (effect on registry-inputs-spec-parity and objectstack check:react-declaration-parity NOT MEASURED); and a lockstep port in objectstack (Q3).",
    "B · build-time derived field. Manifest generation runs the runtime census and publishes a derived accepts-children field. ① Business: same coverage minus the probe's blind spots. ② Long-term: derived from executed code, which is closest to the ruling's 'cannot drift'. But it adds a field to the published Manifest/ManifestComponent type and a DOM render step to both manifest generators; objectstack's runs under plain Node. ③ AI-error: the instrument is measured blind to context and portal registrations (tooltip, sidebar, sidebar-menu-button), so it needs hand-kept context fixtures, which is a hand-kept list again. ④ Startup: a new mechanism plus a new published field. The heaviest option.",
    "C · the flag keeps deciding containment and the debt is paid by declaration (the card's original A/B space). Declaring the flag on this population is refused by objectui#6804. Accepting the false diagnostic (the card's B) was held to be a transition at most. Listed only as the state that holds until Q1 is ruled."
   ],
   "recommendation": "A. It is the only option that covers the measured population without a new published field or a DOM build step. It also dissolves the objectui#6804 conflation instead of papering over it: the `children` input means 'reads a child list' (button, badge, alert and tooltip declare it), and isContainer is left meaning only 'layout container' (Q2). Its residual risk, a hand-declared input drifting from the renderer, is held in both directions by the census that already exists. B is purer on axis ② but fails on ③ (blind spots come back as a hand list) and on ④."
  },
  {
   "question": "Q2. What does isContainer become once it stops deciding containment? react-page.tsx needs a LAYOUT-containment signal that differs from the child-list reading exactly on button/badge/alert, and the flag is a PUBLISHED authoring field: ComponentMeta.isContainer, WidgetManifest.isContainer (the third-party widget path through WidgetRegistry), ComponentMetaSchema ('Can have children'), and the published skills plugin guide ('Accepts child components').",
   "options": [
    "A · keep the field and re-describe it everywhere as 'layout container: excluded from the react-page scope and the public-contract layout ledger', no longer 'can have children'. Drop it from, or re-describe it in, the SDUI manifest. ① Business: react-page keeps Button/Badge/Alert. ② Long-term: one meaning per field. ③ AI-error: a plugin author reading the guide is no longer told the flag admits children. ④ Startup: text-only on types/zod/docs. It touches skills/** (governed), so that part is Tier H.",
    "B · retire the field; react-page switches to an existing signal such as category 'layout'. Whether that set equals today's flagged public set is NOT MEASURED. It removes a published field under the 'retire immediately' default, but it is only safe once that equality is measured.",
    "C · keep the field and its current description. It becomes declared-but-unenforced for containment, against 声明即强制. Not recommended."
   ],
   "recommendation": "A now. Take B only as a follow-up, and only if the category-layout equality is measured."
  },
  {
   "question": "Q3. Cross-repo lockstep. objectstack's save-gate copy of the parser decides not-a-container from the manifest's isContainer, and its lockstep gate cannot see a predicate change (it compares codes and the parse.ts region only). Is the objectui change allowed to land before the objectstack port?",
   "options": [
    "A · port in the same objectui release and pin bump. One objectstack port card is filed, carrying a `Blocked-by:` line on this card. No window where saving and rendering disagree.",
    "B · objectui first, port later. There is a window where the save gate warns (or stays silent) where the renderer does the opposite: the surface-dependent verdict objectstack#12719 exists to prevent."
   ],
   "recommendation": "A. The objectstack#12719 invariant is exactly this case, and its mechanical gate is blind to it."
  }
 ],
 "out_of_scope_findings": [
  "none filed. Every finding of this run sits inside this card's derivation (the ruling put tooltip, the page kinds and the whole containment population in scope), so they are in step1 rather than here. Dedupe words, should the seat split Q3 into an objectstack card: `sdui-lockstep` · `not-a-container` · `isContainer` save gate · `validateJsxPages` manifest containment"
 ]
}

